A verified example from the same training source follows. It is a different task; implement only the new contract.
Example contract: Input is an ASCII string. Return consecutive character runs as [[one-character string,count],...]. Empty input returns []. The returned array may contain up to 256 items.
Shared domain: Numbers are signed 64-bit integers; all required intermediate and final arithmetic fits i64. Arrays contain at most 64 items and strings are ASCII with at most 256 bytes, unless an explicit task exception says otherwise. Whitespace means space, TAB, LF, FF or CR. JSON booleans and null retain their normal types.
Example solution:
```rust
use serde_json::{Value, json};

pub fn solve(input: Value) -> Value {
    let input_str = input.as_str().unwrap();
    let mut result = Vec::new();
    let mut chars = input_str.chars().peekable();

    while let Some(c) = chars.next() {
        let mut count = 1;
        while chars.peek() == Some(&c) {
            chars.next();
            count += 1;
        }
        result.push(json!([c.to_string(), count]));
    }

    json!(result)
}
```
New task:
